Hello VoxelMatters team,

you covered Solidon3D in detail on 22 August, so this is not a second product
introduction, only what has genuinely been added since the state you
described.

At the time, the result of an edit was a triangle mesh. As of 0.5.0 it is an
exact body: holes, countersinks and threads stay real faces and edges when
they are moved, duplicated or removed, and STEP goes in and comes back out
cleanly.

The part that changes the story rather than extending the feature list is that
the choice disappeared. Two geometry kernels run under one interface, and the
options that used to switch between them have been removed. The user does not
decide between two modes; Solidon keeps the exact body as long as it can and
says so when it cannot. Existing projects keep computing unchanged.

That also closes a gap your readers will recognise. CAD packages accept a
downloaded STL, but their tools do not grip triangles, so the part gets
rebuilt. The mesh tools that could edit it are discontinued. Parametric
customisers only work when the original author anticipated the parameter. The
most common job in printing, adapting a downloaded part so it fits something,
has been without a proper tool for years.

The second addition is the one that photographs well. A honeycomb pattern or a
knurl used to fill the object tree with hundreds of faces. It is now one
feature with pitch, cell width and depth, removed with a click and reapplied
with three numbers, including around a handle, where it is measured in the
unrolled surface so the grooves are equally deep and the circumference closes
without a seam.

Shorter items: dimensions are edited on the model itself with a live preview
instead of in a dialog placed in front of it; an existing thread gets its
counterpart on the mating part at the press of a button, in a table size;
resin printers are supported; and a model with open areas is closed while it
is read instead of merely reported.

The demo is free and complete until the end of October, so nothing is needed
from me to look at any of this. Print-resolution images on request.

Solidon3D runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, in six languages, with no account
and no network.

